-- §6.2 T4 — THE OPEN HALF, and the ONLY in-tree test of the #1155 (F-3c)
-- closedness gate. Identical to s6-2-t3-closed-goal-reported.mdk except for ONE
-- TOKEN on the last line: `[1]` becomes `[]`.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived, NOT captured): the goal is now
-- `Sh (Q Int Bool (List ?e))` with `?e` an unbound metavariable that nothing in
-- the program determines and no scheme quantifies (`main` is not generalized).
-- Both impls still MATCH it -- each carries a bare variable at argument 2
-- (`z`), and `matchStep`'s `TyVar` arm binds any mono including an open one --
-- and they are still ⊑-incomparable, so the selector still reaches its
-- no-unique-minimum arm with the SAME candidate set as the sibling.
--
-- But §6.2 **T4** says a goal not closed at the point `inst` is applied is
-- DEFERRED, not decided, and §6 C1 declines to quantify over it in as many
-- words: "a C1 quantified over commitment sites would reject it, while T4 and T6
-- say it is well-formed and that deferral is the whole point." Committing there
-- is a T3 violation on the implementation's part, not a C1 violation on the
-- program's. So the goal-site reject MUST NOT fire here, and the row asserts
-- `!T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE`.
--
-- ✅ SINCE F-3d (#614/#311) THE GATE DECIDES THIS FILE'S VERDICT. Condition (a) is
-- demoted to a `W-INCOMPARABLE-IMPLS` WARNING, so the declaration no longer rejects
-- anything here: this file now ACCEPTS (exit 0) while its one-token sibling still
-- REJECTS with T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE. The pair discriminates on VERDICT.
--
-- ⚠️ THE NEGATIVE ASSERTION IS STILL LOAD-BEARING AND MUST STAY. Verdict alone is a
-- WEAKER test than the code assertion, not a replacement for it: delete the
-- closedness gate and this file starts REJECTING, which a verdict pin catches -- but
-- ANY new reject on this file catches that too, so verdict alone cannot say the
-- reject came from the min⊑ arm. `!T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE` names the exact diagnostic
-- the gate suppresses, which is the only assertion that distinguishes "the closedness
-- gate was removed" from "something else started rejecting". Making this row
-- positive-only would make it inert against the regression it exists for.
--
-- ⚠️ THE VALUE THIS FILE NOW PRINTS IS DECIDED BY DECLARATION ORDER -- see #1183,
-- and do NOT read `1` as a correct answer. The two impls are ⊑-incomparable, so
-- `pickMostSpecificEntry` has no minimum and commits to the HEAD of the candidate
-- list; swap the two `impl` blocks and this prints `2`, at exit 0, on both engines.
-- §6.2 T4's actual answer is to DEFER a non-closed goal to quiescence and decide it
-- there, which this compiler has no pass for (§11's T3/T4 row records the
-- divergence). F-3d makes that pre-existing residue USER-REACHABLE and warns on it
-- rather than silently accepting it -- the explicit trade in #311's owner decision.
-- Section 4 of the gate carries it as a KNOWN-BAD permutation row.
data Q a b c = Q a b c

interface Sh a where
  sh : a -> Int

impl Sh (Q Int y z) where
  sh _ = 1

impl Sh (Q x Bool z) where
  sh _ = 2

main = println (sh (Q 1 True []))
